    question: 'Do you work with Sonos, Control4, or Crestron?',
    answer: "Yes. Sonos is a central part of many of the whole-house audio systems we install and integrates well with most smart home platforms. For AV control systems, we work with platforms appropriate for the project scope — from Sonos-native control for simpler setups to more comprehensive control systems for larger homes. We recommend what's right for your home and budget, not what has the highest margin.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you automate an existing home or is this only for new construction?',
    answer: "Both. New construction is ideal because we can pre-wire for everything before the walls close. But we work in finished homes across San Antonio regularly. Wireless control systems and smart switches eliminate most of the need for new wire runs in existing homes. We'll assess your home and tell you what's practical and what isn't.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you program the system and train us how to use it?',
    answer: "Yes. Programming and a homeowner walkthrough are part of every smart home install we do. A system you can't figure out is a system you won't use. We program scenes, label everything clearly, and walk you through every function before we leave. If something's not right after you've lived with it, call us.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What does a smart home AV system cost in San Antonio?',
    answer: "Entry-level smart home AV systems — a unified control interface for your TV, audio, and streaming — start around $3,000–$8,000 installed. Whole-home systems with multi-room audio, theater integration, and lighting control typically run $15,000–$50,000 depending on scope and equipment tier. We provide detailed, itemized quotes at no charge.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Is smart home automation worth it?',
    answer: "Yes — if the system is designed correctly. The value is in daily convenience: one button or one voice command controls your TV, audio, lighting, and streaming instead of managing four different remotes and three apps. Systems that fail are ones that were over-engineered for how the homeowner actually lives, or programmed and left without a proper walkthrough. A well-designed, well-programmed smart home system gets used every single day. A poorly designed one collects dust. We design for how you actually live.",
